  "summary": [
    "Forward Kinematics (FK) computes end-effector position from joint angles, while Inverse Kinematics (IK) solves for joint angles given a 3D target position.",
    "Analytical Two-Bone IK uses the Law of Cosines to solve shoulder and elbow angles in closed form with constant O(1) complexity and sub-millimeter precision.",
    "Cyclic Coordinate Descent (CCD) iteratively rotates joints backward from tip to root using vector dot and cross products, handling arbitrary chain lengths.",
    "FABRIK (Forward And Backward Reaching Inverse Kinematics) operates on pure 3D points and Euclidean line projections, converging in 2-4 iterations with zero trigonometric overhead.",
    "Joint angle limits and Pole Vectors constrain degrees of freedom, preventing unnatural hyperextension and locking elbow/knee swivel orientation."
  ],
